{"id": "2026-10-12-news-riyadh-airport-struck-again", "date": "2026-10-12", "stream": "news", "headline": "Riyadh airport struck again", "summary": "A projectile struck King Khalid International Airport in Riyadh on the evening of Sunday 11 October, killing a Pakistani resident and injuring 11, Saudi Arabia's civil aviation authority said, a day after the deadliest strike on the airport so far.", "url": "https://news.un.org/en/story/2026/10/1168572", "source_domain": "news.un.org", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 3, "display_body": "A projectile struck King Khalid International Airport in Riyadh on the evening of Sunday 11 October, killing a Pakistani resident and injuring 11, Saudi Arabia's civil aviation authority said, a day after the deadliest strike on the airport so far. A witness on a taxiing plane told Reuters it hit the tarmac next to parked aircraft at about 20:30 local time and started a fire, and Al Jazeera says the strike was on terminals 3 and 4, which serve domestic flights. India's embassy said one of its citizens died of injuries from Saturday's attack, which raises that toll to 13. Al Jazeera counts four strikes on the airport in five days, and the airport has been closed since Saturday. The Houthis claimed the earlier strikes, but Al Jazeera reports no claim yet for Sunday's. UN Secretary-General António Guterres said late on Saturday that attacks on civilians and civilian infrastructure violate international law and \"must cease immediately\". On Monday 12 October Australia and New Zealand joined the US, Canada, the UK, South Korea and Japan in warning their citizens against travel to the kingdom.", "why": "Gulf aviation is now a regular target, and Western governments are telling their citizens to stay away. That makes it more likely that the US joins the war in Yemen, which President Trump says he is weighing.", "thread_id": "2026-10-12-news-riyadh-airport-struck-again", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-12", "event_date": "2026-10-11", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Saudi Arabia", "Houthis", "King Khalid International Airport", "Yemen"]}
{"id": "2026-10-12-news-trump-claims-russia-ukraine-energy-truce", "date": "2026-10-12", "stream": "news", "headline": "Trump claims Russia-Ukraine energy truce", "summary": "US President Donald Trump announced on Sunday 11 October that Russia and Ukraine had agreed to an immediate \"energy ceasefire\", but neither Moscow nor Kyiv had confirmed any such deal, and Russian strikes on Ukrainian power infrastructure continued.", "url": "https://www.rts.ch/info/monde/2026/article/guerre-en-ukraine-trump-annonce-un-cessez-le-feu-kiev-dement-29382545.html", "source_domain": "rts.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "US President Donald Trump announced on Sunday 11 October that Russia and Ukraine had agreed to an immediate \"energy ceasefire\", but neither Moscow nor Kyiv had confirmed any such deal, and Russian strikes on Ukrainian power infrastructure continued. \"Effective immediately, there is an ENERGY CEASEFIRE… Both parties have agreed. Do not break it!\", Trump wrote on Truth Social, without giving any details. Volodymyr Zelensky told Axios within minutes that the announcement was \"news for me\". He said Ukraine would accept a halt if Russia did, and asked Washington to say when it starts. Later the same day he said a drone had hit a Kyiv power plant and caused blackouts in the capital. Moscow has not commented. The claim follows Trump's deal on Friday 9 October to buy Russian diesel to lower fuel prices before the US midterms. DW notes that the deal undercuts US sanctions on Russian energy that Trump signed only weeks ago. Zelensky first offered a mutual halt to energy strikes in September.", "why": "the claim has no confirmation from either side, and Trump has spent the week pressing Kyiv to stop hitting Russian refineries. It reads as a demand on Ukraine more than an agreement, until Moscow confirms it and the strikes stop.", "thread_id": "2026-10-12-news-trump-claims-russia-ukraine-energy-truce", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-12", "event_date": "2026-10-11", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Donald Trump", "Volodymyr Zelensky", "Russia", "Ukraine"]}
{"id": "2026-10-12-news-slovenes-reject-jan-a-inquiry-law-change", "date": "2026-10-12", "stream": "news", "headline": "Slovenes reject Janša inquiry-law change", "summary": "Slovenian voters narrowly rejected the Janez Janša government's amendment to the Parliamentary Inquiry Act in a binding referendum on Sunday 11 October: 51.3% against and 48.7% for, with 99.97% of votes counted, according to the State Election Commission.", "url": "https://volitve.dvk-rs.si/referendum-preiskava/", "source_domain": "volitve.dvk-rs.si", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["new source"], "topics": ["politics", "world"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Slovenian voters narrowly rejected the Janez Janša government's amendment to the Parliamentary Inquiry Act in a binding referendum on Sunday 11 October: 51.3% against and 48.7% for, with 99.97% of votes counted, according to the State Election Commission. The count stands at 471,859 against and 448,643 for, on a turnout of 54.6% of about 1.7 million voters. The amendment would have obliged people summoned by parliamentary commissions of inquiry to appear at once, without first being able to challenge the summons in court before the commission finished its work. The opposition called this a \"political police\". Three consultative questions were on the same ballot, on social assistance, voting rights for foreigners and the RTV licence fee. The licence-fee question was decided by fewer than 700 votes. Janša returned to power after the liberal former prime minister Robert Golob failed to form a government, even though Golob's party narrowly won the March election.", "why": "it is the first national vote against the new government's programme, which includes cutting public-sector jobs and funding for civil-rights NGOs. The opposition presented the vote as a test of Slovenia's democratic resilience.", "thread_id": "2026-10-12-news-slovenes-reject-jan-a-inquiry-law-change", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-12", "event_date": "2026-10-11", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Slovenia", "Janez Janša", "State Election Commission"]}
{"id": "2026-10-12-news-air-strikes-kill-gold-miners-near-halayeb", "date": "2026-10-12", "stream": "news", "headline": "Air strikes kill gold miners near Halayeb", "summary": "At least 72 artisanal gold miners were killed in air strikes on Saturday 10 October in the disputed Halayeb border area between Sudan and Egypt, the Sudan-based rights group Emergency Lawyers said on Sunday 11 October, accusing the Egyptian air force.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/air-strikes-kill-dozens-of-gold-miners-on-sudan-egypt-border/a-79634889", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "At least 72 artisanal gold miners were killed in air strikes on Saturday 10 October in the disputed Halayeb border area between Sudan and Egypt, the Sudan-based rights group Emergency Lawyers said on Sunday 11 October, accusing the Egyptian air force. The strikes hit rugged ground south of the Halayeb triangle, east of the Nile, which both Cairo and Khartoum claim and which is known for illegal gold mining and smuggling. Egypt has not commented. It has previously acknowledged a \"large-scale operation\" against illegal mining inside its borders, after similar strikes on mining sites in the same area in June. The remote location makes independent verification hard, and a witness quoted by DW said nobody knows exactly how many died. Chatham House estimates that up to 60% of Sudan's unofficial gold exports end up in Egypt, and that trade helps fund the Rapid Support Forces (RSF), the paramilitary fighting Sudan's army.", "why": "if the attribution holds, Egypt, which backs Sudan's army, is using lethal force against the cross-border gold trade that helps finance the war.", "thread_id": "2026-10-12-news-air-strikes-kill-gold-miners-near-halayeb", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-12", "event_date": "2026-10-10", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Sudan", "Egypt", "Halayeb triangle", "Emergency Lawyers"]}
{"id": "2026-10-12-news-b-rbel-bas-quits-german-cabinet-and-spd-lead", "date": "2026-10-12", "stream": "news", "headline": "Bärbel Bas quits German cabinet and SPD lead", "summary": "German Labour Minister Bärbel Bas resigned on Monday 12 October as both minister and co-leader of the Social Democrats (SPD).", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/international/deutschland-spd-co-chefin-baerbel-bas-tritt-zurueck", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["politics", "world"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "German Labour Minister Bärbel Bas resigned on Monday 12 October as both minister and co-leader of the Social Democrats (SPD). She cited the death of her stepdaughter and public attacks \"also from the ranks of the coalition partner\", Chancellor Friedrich Merz's CDU/CSU. \"As I currently cannot muster the strength to fully carry out my government and party work at short notice, I have decided to give up both offices,\" she wrote in a letter circulated by the SPD leadership. SRF's foreign-desk head called the statement a sharp accusation against the chancellor. The CDU had accused her of stalling pension and labour-law reforms. The SPD now needs a new labour minister and a new co-leader alongside Lars Klingbeil.", "why": "losing one of the SPD's two leaders over open coalition friction weakens Merz's government just as it tries to push through pension reform.", "thread_id": "2026-10-12-news-b-rbel-bas-quits-german-cabinet-and-spd-lead", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-12", "event_date": "2026-10-12", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Bärbel Bas", "SPD", "Friedrich Merz", "Germany"]}
{"id": "2026-10-12-news-north-korea-fires-two-ballistic-missiles", "date": "2026-10-12", "stream": "news", "headline": "North Korea fires two ballistic missiles", "summary": "North Korea fired at least two ballistic missiles into the Sea of Japan on the morning of Monday 12 October, one of which flew more than 900 km, according to the South Korean and Japanese militaries.", "url": "https://www.japantimes.co.jp/news/2026/10/12/japan/north-korea-multiple-ballistic-missiles-launch/", "source_domain": "japantimes.co.jp", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "North Korea fired at least two ballistic missiles into the Sea of Japan on the morning of Monday 12 October, one of which flew more than 900 km, according to the South Korean and Japanese militaries. Japan's Defence Ministry said the first missile, launched at about 09:19 local time, flew about 220 km to a peak altitude of 20 km. The second, launched at 09:36, flew more than 910 km at a peak of 70 km. Both landed outside Japan's exclusive economic zone. Deputy Defence Minister Takashi Koyari said the second missile followed an \"irregular\" path, which suggests a possible hypersonic weapon (one that can manoeuvre at more than five times the speed of sound, which makes it harder to intercept). The launch came after a South Korean hypersonic test, and after a single ballistic launch by Pyongyang on 3 October.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-10-12-news-north-korea-fires-two-ballistic-missiles", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-12", "event_date": "2026-10-12", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["North Korea", "Japan", "South Korea"]}
{"id": "2026-10-12-news-maillard-motion-targets-premium-rises", "date": "2026-10-12", "stream": "news", "headline": "Maillard motion targets premium rises", "summary": "Pierre-Yves Maillard, a Socialist member of the Council of States, wants any average rise in mandatory health-insurance premiums above 3% to need approval by Parliament and be open to an optional referendum. The Federal Council recommended rejecting his motion on 9 September.", "url": "https://www.parlament.ch/fr/ratsbetrieb/suche-curia-vista/geschaeft?AffairId=20263740", "source_domain": "parlament.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "health"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Pierre-Yves Maillard, a Socialist member of the Council of States, wants any average rise in mandatory health-insurance premiums above 3% to need approval by Parliament and be open to an optional referendum. The Federal Council recommended rejecting his motion on 9 September. Motion 26.3740 was filed on 18 June. It would require both chambers to ratify the new premiums each autumn session when the average rise exceeds 3%. If a referendum is called, premiums would be frozen until the vote. If voters say no, the government could raise them only in line with consumer prices, and by less than 3%. The average rise of about 5% announced for 2027 would already have crossed that threshold, RTS notes. In its reply the Federal Council says premiums track costs, so the mechanism \"would have no effect\" on the growth of health spending while seriously disrupting the insurance system. Centrist national councillor Benjamin Roduit argues that voters who reject a rise would also have to say which benefits to cut, which risks a deadlock. RTS says the Council of States could debate the motion in December.", "why": "premiums are a large and rising cost for every household in Switzerland. The motion would give voters a direct check on them, but the government says it would treat the symptom rather than the cost growth behind it.", "thread_id": "2026-10-12-news-maillard-motion-targets-premium-rises", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-12", "event_date": "2026-09-09", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Pierre-Yves Maillard", "Federal Council", "Council of States", "LAMal"]}
{"id": "2026-10-12-news-strasbourg-takes-vaud-ex-spouse-tax-case", "date": "2026-10-12", "stream": "news", "headline": "Strasbourg takes Vaud ex-spouse tax case", "summary": "The European Court of Human Rights has declared admissible a complaint that Vaud's rules making ex-spouses jointly liable for tax discriminate against women, Le Temps reported on Monday 12 October.", "url": "https://www.letemps.ch/articles/exclusif-la-cour-europeenne-des-droits-de-l-homme-dira-si-la-solidarite-fiscale-entre-ex-epoux-est-discriminatoire-ou-non", "source_domain": "letemps.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["switzerland"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The European Court of Human Rights has declared admissible a complaint that Vaud's rules making ex-spouses jointly liable for tax discriminate against women, Le Temps reported on Monday 12 October. The case was brought by Lausanne lawyer and Green national councillor Raphaël Mahaim. In 2024 he won the climate case in which the Strasbourg court found that Switzerland had violated the rights of the Senior Women for Climate Protection. Only about 10% of applications get past the admissibility stage, the paper notes. Mahaim calls it \"a first small victory\". If the court finds against Switzerland, the situation of some Vaud women held liable for their former husbands' tax debts could be reviewed, and similar rules in Appenzell and elsewhere in Europe could change.", "why": "a ruling against Switzerland would force a review of who carries a divorced couple's tax debts, which can fall on the ex-wife long after separation.", "thread_id": "2026-10-12-news-strasbourg-takes-vaud-ex-spouse-tax-case", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-12", "event_date": null, "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["European Court of Human Rights", "Vaud", "Raphaël Mahaim"]}
{"id": "2026-10-12-news-bern-plans-auctions-for-wine-imports", "date": "2026-10-12", "stream": "news", "headline": "Bern plans auctions for wine imports", "summary": "Economics Minister Guy Parmelin and the Federal Office for Agriculture plan to auction wine import quotas, which would make foreign wine more expensive and favour Swiss producers, SRF reported on Monday 12 October.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/wirtschaft/hilfe-fuer-schweizer-winzer-weinhaendler-kritisiert-parmelins-schnapsidee", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "Economics Minister Guy Parmelin and the Federal Office for Agriculture plan to auction wine import quotas, which would make foreign wine more expensive and favour Swiss producers, SRF reported on Monday 12 October. An earlier plan to allow imports only for traders who also buy Swiss grapes was rejected in consultation in the spring. Under the new plan, importers would bid for tariff quotas, and the highest bidders would get them. The office's deputy director, Jean-Marc Chappuis, said not to expect too much from it, because the real problem is that Swiss people, especially younger ones, are drinking less wine while vineyard area has stayed the same, leading to overproduction. Wine merchant Philipp Schwander called it a \"crackpot idea\" (Schnapsidee) that was rejected 20 years ago, and said Switzerland exports only 1.4% of its wine, compared with 40–60% for Germany, Italy and Spain. The proposal now goes to consultation.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-10-12-news-bern-plans-auctions-for-wine-imports", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-12", "event_date": null, "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Guy Parmelin", "Federal Office for Agriculture"]}
{"id": "2026-10-12-news-cassis-heads-to-armenia-and-georgia", "date": "2026-10-12", "stream": "news", "headline": "Cassis heads to Armenia and Georgia", "summary": "Foreign Minister Ignazio Cassis, who chairs the OSCE this year, will visit Armenia and Georgia from Wednesday 14 to Friday 16 October, the Federal Department of Foreign Affairs said on Monday 12 October.", "url": "https://www.admin.ch/fr/newnsb/3rmUYv7Xuosy", "source_domain": "admin.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["switzerland", "geopolitics"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "Foreign Minister Ignazio Cassis, who chairs the OSCE this year, will visit Armenia and Georgia from Wednesday 14 to Friday 16 October, the Federal Department of Foreign Affairs said on Monday 12 October. In Yerevan on Thursday 15 October he meets Prime Minister Nikol Pashinyan and Foreign Minister Ararat Mirzoyan to discuss the Armenia–Azerbaijan peace process. Switzerland says it is ready to offer its expertise if both sides want it. In Tbilisi on Friday 16 October he meets Prime Minister Irakli Kobakhidze and Foreign Minister Maka Botchorishvili about the consequences of the 2008 war. There Switzerland also holds a protecting-power mandate, acting as diplomatic go-between for Georgia and Russia. The OSCE co-chairs the Geneva International Discussions on the conflict with the UN and the EU.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-10-12-news-cassis-heads-to-armenia-and-georgia", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-12", "event_date": "2026-10-14", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Ignazio Cassis", "OSCE", "Armenia", "Georgia"]}
